[Black "Example 137- Larsen- B. vs. Uhlmann- W.- Las Palmas (9th matchgame) 1971"]
[Result "1-0"]
[FEN "8/8/p2n3p/P1B1k3/1P2p3/6K1/5P2/8 w - - 0 1"]

 { After some careful calculation, Larsen decided to enter the pawn ending. } 1. { -KEY- } Bxd6+ $1 Kxd6 2. Kf4 Kd5 3. b5 $1 ( { Not } 3. Kf5 $2 h5 $1 4. Kg5 Kc4 { and Black escapes. } ) 
axb5 4. a6 Kc6 5. Kxe4 { It is the f-pawn's turn to run. } ( 5. a7 $2 Kb7 6. Kxe4 Kxa7 7. f4 Kb6 { should obviously be avoided. } ) 
b4 ( { a) Similar play arises after } 5... Kb6 6. f4 ) 
 ( { b) or } 5... h5 6. f4 ) 
6. f4 { Here Black resigned. Let's see a possible conclusion of the game: } b3 7. Kd3 b2 8. Kc2 h5 9. f5 h4 10. f6 h3 11. a7 Kb7 12. f7 ( { A different move order with } 12. a8=Q Kxa8 13. f7 { also works, as noticed by UltraSuperBrainPower. } ) 
h2 13. a8=Q+ $1 { The point - White gains a tempo by promoting with check. } ( { In contrast, } 13. f8=Q $2 { would let Black save himself by the same trick: } b1=Q $1 14. Kxb1 h1=Q ) 
Kxa8 14. f8=Q+ { White wins. } 
 1-0

