 { Here is a game between two of my students, in which White could have triumphed in the pawn race by precise play. } 1. { -KEY- } Kd5 $1 { Obstruction. } ( { In contrast, the game saw both players err: } 1. f4 $2 Kc5 $2 ( { a draw was achieved by } 1. Kd5 Kc6 $1 2. Ke5 Kd7 { or 2...b5 } ) 
2. Ke5 Kc6 ( { after } 2. Kd5 b5 3. f5 b4 4. f6 { , White queens with check } ) 
3. Ke6 $1 b5 ( { or } 3. Kd5 Kc7 4. f5 Kd8 5. Kf7 $1 { and White again queens with check } ) 
4. f5 b4 5. f6 b3 6. f7 b2 7. f8=Q $11 b1=Q $11 8. Qc8 { and Black resigned. } ) 
Ka4 ( { If instead } 1... Ka6 { going for the f-pawn, then after } 2. f4 Kb7 3. f5 Kc7 4. Ke6 $1 { White wins, as we already saw in the previous note. } ) 
2. f4 b5 3. f5 b4 4. Kc4 $1 ( { But not } 4. f6 $2 b3 5. f7 b2 6. f8=Q $11 b1=Q $11 7. Qa8 Kb3 8. Qb7 Ka2 9. Qxb1 Kxb1 10. Ke6 Kc2 { and the black king reaches the f-file in the end. } ) 
b3 5. Kc3 $1 { Luring the black king to the 'mined' a3-square, in similar fashion to Smyslov's play in the previous example. } Ka3 6. f6 b2 7. f7 b1=Q 8. f8=Q+ Ka4 ( { Or } 8... Ka2 9. Qa8 ) 
9. Qa8+ Kb5 10. Qb8+ { White wins. } 
