[FEN "8/5pk1/2bp1q2/2p2p2/2P2P2/1NQP4/2K5/8 b - - 0 1"]

 { Relying on an extra pawn Lautier takes the opportunity to transpose to a pawn ending, perhaps already at this point anticipating a future race of passed pawns. } 1... { -KEY- } Ba4 $1 2. d4 { The best try. } ( { a) After } 2. Qa5 Qa1 $1 ) 
 ( { b) or } 2. Kd2 Qxc3 3. Kxc3 Bxb3 4. Kxb3 Kg6 5. Ka4 Kh5 6. Kb5 Kg4 7. Kc6 Kxf4 8. Kxd6 Ke3 { , followed by 9...f4, Black wins with less effort - Lautier. } ) 
Qxd4 3. Qxd4+ cxd4 4. Kb2 Bxb3 $1 { Setting the scene for a pawn race. } ( { Less convincing is } 4... d3 $6 5. Nd2 Bc6 ( { or } 5... Kg6 6. Kc3 Bc2 7. Nf1 { , heading for e3; } ) 
6. Kc3 Be4 7. Nf1 $1 Kg6 8. Ng3 { with a barrier. } ) 
5. Kxb3 { This position has been used as a blindfold exercise in my classes, with Black to play and win. If you are up for a little challenge, try it out - it is about one, long line without any subvariations until the very end. } Kg6 ( { A different plan with } 5... Kf6 { also works, e.g. } 6. Kc2 Ke6 7. Kd3 ( { after } 6... Kg6 6. Kd2 d5 8. c5 Kd7 9. Kd3 Kc6 10. Kxd4 f6 $1 { the value of the extra pawn is again visible } ) 
Kd7 8. Kxd4 Kc6 9. Kd3 Kc5 $1 10. Kc3 d5 11. cxd5 Kxd5 12. Kd3 f6 { . This line highlights the fact that any kind of extra pawn, even a "bad" one like the one on f7 in this example, acquires more importance once a pure pawn ending is reached. } ) 
 ( { Equally possible is } 5... Kf8 { in similar fashion to the previous line, as observed by DraggonZ. } ) 
6. Kc2 Kh5 7. Kd3 Kg4 8. Kxd4 Kxf4 9. Kd5 Ke3 $1 { As often occurs in pawn races, the king move releasing the pawn's advance is the key to success. } ( { a) Both } 9... Kg4 $2 10. Kxd6 f4 11. c5 f3 12. c6 f2 13. c7 f1=Q $11 14. c8=Q ) 
 ( { b) and } 9... Kg3 $2 10. Kxd6 f4 11. c5 f3 12. c6 f2 13. c7 f1=Q $11 14. c8=Q $11 Qf6 15. Kd5 $1 { would fail the test; } ) 
 ( { c) but } 9... Kg5 $5 { with a check on f6 at the end, also works. } ) 
10. Kxd6 f4 11. c5 f3 12. c6 f2 13. c7 f1=Q 14. c8=Q Qf6+ $1 { The final detail. } ( { The exchange of queens is inevitable, e.g. } 14... Qf6 15. Kd5 Qd4 16. Kc6 Qc4 { , so White resigned. } ) 

 0-1

